cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "toolfault: a fault-injection benchmark for tool-using LLM agents"
message: "If you use this benchmark, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Vasileios
    family-names: Balafas
    email: me@vasilis.pw
    affiliation: "Independent"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mpvasilis/toolfault"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-01"
keywords:
  - large language models
  - agents
  - tool use
  - reliability
  - fault injection
  - calibration
  - selective prediction
abstract: >-
  A benchmark that measures whether a tool-using agent's own confidence signal
  carries information on the episodes where a tool returned plausible wrong data,
  and what an escalation threshold built on that signal costs. Thirteen faults in
  three families (loud, silent, adversarial) are injected one per episode against a
  deterministic synthetic corporate registry. Ground truth is derived from world
  state by pure functions, with no LLM judge in the scoring path. Calibration is
  reported per fault condition rather than in aggregate. A hand-written reference
  policy resolves all fourteen conditions, so the ceiling is known and enforced in
  continuous integration, and degenerate baselines are published to show the
  benchmark cannot be scored without doing the verification work.
